1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,251
Look at these two branching shapes. On the left, each fork splits in two, then in two

2
00:00:05,251 --> 00:00:09,019
again. On the right, one fork spreads straight into six tips.

3
00:00:09,019 --> 00:00:14,208
Could a symmetry score mistake a tree made from these for a perfectly balanced tree?

4
00:00:14,600 --> 00:00:17,642
These are rooted trees. The top point is the root.

5
00:00:17,642 --> 00:00:21,718
Each line leads to a child, and a point with no children is a leaf.

6
00:00:21,718 --> 00:00:26,768
The picture records branching relationships, not the physical lengths of the lines.

7
00:00:27,167 --> 00:00:30,216
Here, fully symmetric has a precise meaning.

8
00:00:30,216 --> 00:00:34,373
At every fork, all the child trees must have the same shape.

9
00:00:34,373 --> 00:00:37,422
Both of these separate trees pass that test.

10
00:00:37,422 --> 00:00:40,679
But their shapes are different from each other.

11
00:00:41,067 --> 00:00:46,619
Now give every point a weight. For our first example, use the number of children plus

12
00:00:46,619 --> 00:00:50,735
one. A leaf weighs one. A point with two children weighs three.

13
00:00:50,735 --> 00:00:53,283
A point with six children weighs seven.

14
00:00:53,667 --> 00:00:59,109
Add the weights on the left. There are three branching points, each worth three, and

15
00:00:59,109 --> 00:01:03,579
four leaves, each worth one. Three plus six plus four makes thirteen.

16
00:01:03,579 --> 00:01:06,171
We will call this sum the weighted size.

17
00:01:06,567 --> 00:01:11,236
On the right, the root weighs seven and the six leaves contribute six.

18
00:01:11,236 --> 00:01:16,706
That also makes thirteen. Different shapes have landed on exactly the same number.

19
00:01:16,706 --> 00:01:19,575
This is a collision between weighted sizes.

20
00:01:19,967 --> 00:01:24,938
Build a new tree by putting these two trees under a new root.

21
00:01:24,938 --> 00:01:28,362
Its left child has weighted size thirteen.

22
00:01:28,362 --> 00:01:32,274
Its right child also has weighted size thirteen.

23
00:01:32,274 --> 00:01:36,431
So a comparison of the two sizes sees no imbalance.

24
00:01:36,800 --> 00:01:40,527
Inside each child, every fork was already symmetric.

25
00:01:40,527 --> 00:01:43,825
Every local comparison therefore returns zero.

26
00:01:43,825 --> 00:01:49,917
Adding all those zeros still gives zero, even though the new root joins two different

27
00:01:49,917 --> 00:01:52,856
shapes. You can see the mistake directly.

28
00:01:53,233 --> 00:01:56,798
A Colless-like index adds these local differences.

29
00:01:56,798 --> 00:02:01,718
It is called sound if zero happens exactly for fully symmetric trees.

30
00:02:01,718 --> 00:02:05,283
Our example breaks soundness for this weight rule.

31
00:02:05,283 --> 00:02:10,345
Soundness concerns the zero score, not the ranking of every other tree.

32
00:02:10,733 --> 00:02:16,079
Perhaps we only chose bad weights. Could larger whole numbers avoid every collision?

33
00:02:16,079 --> 00:02:21,425
The theorem says no. Whatever nonnegative whole-number weights you choose, some pair

34
00:02:21,425 --> 00:02:24,989
of different symmetric trees will share a weighted size.

35
00:02:25,367 --> 00:02:29,944
To see why, first describe a symmetric tree by its branching recipe.

36
00:02:29,944 --> 00:02:35,667
The list two, three means two children at the first level, then three children at the

37
00:02:35,667 --> 00:02:38,831
next. There are two times three, or six leaves.

38
00:02:39,200 --> 00:02:45,230
Reverse that recipe to three, two. The shape changes: three branches come first, rather

39
00:02:45,230 --> 00:02:48,141
than two. But the leaf count is still six.

40
00:02:48,141 --> 00:02:53,408
Multiplication forgets the order of the recipe, while the tree remembers it.

41
00:02:53,800 --> 00:02:57,444
For the counting proof, use the numbers two, three and four.

42
00:02:57,444 --> 00:03:00,421
With one of each, there are six different orders.

43
00:03:00,421 --> 00:03:05,644
Every order gives twenty-four leaves. These are six different symmetric trees with the

44
00:03:05,644 --> 00:03:06,616
same leaf count.

45
00:03:07,000 --> 00:03:11,689
Next take two copies of each number. There are ninety different recipes.

46
00:03:11,689 --> 00:03:17,029
In general, take m copies of each. Their leaf count is twenty-four to the power m.

47
00:03:17,029 --> 00:03:20,416
We need to count how many distinct recipes share it.

48
00:03:20,800 --> 00:03:26,599
If all three m positions were different, there would be three m factorial arrangements.

49
00:03:26,599 --> 00:03:29,265
Factorial means multiplying down to one.

50
00:03:29,265 --> 00:03:34,398
But the m copies of two are interchangeable, and so are the threes and fours.

51
00:03:34,398 --> 00:03:36,664
Divide by m factorial three times.

52
00:03:37,033 --> 00:03:40,403
This exact count is not twenty-seven to the power m.

53
00:03:40,403 --> 00:03:46,301
That number counts every string of length three m using three symbols, including unbalanced

54
00:03:46,301 --> 00:03:51,745
mixtures. Our balanced mixture is the largest group when strings are sorted by their

55
00:03:51,745 --> 00:03:53,041
three symbol counts.

56
00:03:53,433 --> 00:03:56,452
There are only a quadratic number of those groups.

57
00:03:56,452 --> 00:04:01,705
So our count is at least twenty-seven to the power m, divided by that quadratic factor.

58
00:04:01,705 --> 00:04:06,897
The factor slows growth, but it cannot permanently defeat the larger exponential base.

59
00:04:07,267 --> 00:04:09,751
Now count the possible weighted sizes.

60
00:04:09,751 --> 00:04:15,439
First remove the leaves. Every tree in the family has exactly the same number, so their

61
00:04:15,439 --> 00:04:20,931
total leaf weight is identical. Subtracting that common contribution preserves every

62
00:04:20,931 --> 00:04:22,435
equality between sizes.

63
00:04:22,833 --> 00:04:28,019
What remains is the internal sum. Each point there has two, three or four children.

64
00:04:28,019 --> 00:04:30,768
Let M be the largest of those three weights.

65
00:04:30,768 --> 00:04:34,953
The number of internal points is smaller than the number of leaves.

66
00:04:35,333 --> 00:04:39,097
You can see this bound by looking upwards from the leaves.

67
00:04:39,097 --> 00:04:43,184
Each earlier level has at most half as many points as the next.

68
00:04:43,184 --> 00:04:48,245
Even adding every internal level gives fewer points than the leaf level alone.

69
00:04:48,633 --> 00:04:54,366
The internal sum is therefore a whole number between zero and M times twenty-four to

70
00:04:54,366 --> 00:04:58,119
the power m. Think of those integers as numbered boxes.

71
00:04:58,119 --> 00:05:02,145
Each recipe must go into the box matching its internal sum.

72
00:05:02,533 --> 00:05:07,047
When there are more recipes than boxes, two recipes must share a box.

73
00:05:07,047 --> 00:05:12,608
Here is that pigeonhole principle on a small illustration: four objects cannot occupy

74
00:05:12,608 --> 00:05:18,037
three boxes without a collision. The proof applies the same idea to enormous trees.

75
00:05:18,433 --> 00:05:21,827
For a concrete comparison, set M equal to five.

76
00:05:21,827 --> 00:05:27,242
The graph shows the exact recipe count divided by the upper bound on boxes.

77
00:05:27,242 --> 00:05:33,668
Above one, a collision is forced. The count eventually crosses because twenty-seven grows

78
00:05:33,668 --> 00:05:35,401
faster than twenty-four.

79
00:05:35,800 --> 00:05:38,733
That crossing does not rely on M being five.

80
00:05:38,733 --> 00:05:41,599
Any fixed choice of M is eventually beaten.

81
00:05:41,599 --> 00:05:47,598
Two different symmetric trees then have the same internal sum, the same leaf contribution,

82
00:05:47,598 --> 00:05:50,464
and therefore the same total weighted size.

83
00:05:50,833 --> 00:05:54,344
Join the colliding trees under one root, just as before.

84
00:05:54,344 --> 00:05:59,046
Every local comparison gives zero, but the root has different child shapes.

85
00:05:59,046 --> 00:06:04,438
This completes a hand proof for all nonnegative integer weight functions, not just the

86
00:06:04,438 --> 00:06:06,193
examples a computer checked.

87
00:06:06,567 --> 00:06:12,346
The precise theorem is stronger. The weights at two, three and four only need to be whole

88
00:06:12,346 --> 00:06:17,866
multiples of one positive unit. Divide by that unit and the boxes are integers again.

89
00:06:17,866 --> 00:06:19,815
Rational weights are included.

90
00:06:20,200 --> 00:06:22,579
The leaf weight need not fit that unit.

91
00:06:22,579 --> 00:06:25,080
It cancels because the leaf counts agree.

92
00:06:25,080 --> 00:06:28,619
Other branching weights do not enter this counting family.

93
00:06:28,619 --> 00:06:34,048
These restrictions matter: the result does not say that every possible weight rule fails.

94
00:06:34,433 --> 00:06:38,764
Mir, Rotger and Rossello proposed the whole-number conjecture in 2018.

95
00:06:38,764 --> 00:06:44,207
They already gave some collisions, including one for powers of two, and sound irrational

96
00:06:44,207 --> 00:06:49,465
weight rules. This result is the universal impossibility proof, not the first example

97
00:06:49,465 --> 00:06:50,393
of a collision.

98
00:06:50,767 --> 00:06:56,286
What about weights built from two independent units, such as one and the square root

99
00:06:56,286 --> 00:07:02,201
of two? Then our boxes become a grid. This counting argument no longer forces a collision.

100
00:07:02,201 --> 00:07:05,815
Whether some such rule is sound remains unsettled here.

101
00:07:06,200 --> 00:07:10,572
Remember the three steps. Different symmetric trees can share a size.

102
00:07:10,572 --> 00:07:16,210
Equal sizes hide an asymmetric join. And counting forces that collision for every allowed

103
00:07:16,210 --> 00:07:21,659
whole-number rule. Correctness confidence is high; originality confidence is moderate,

104
00:07:21,659 --> 00:07:24,320
because the literature search was bounded.

105
00:07:24,700 --> 00:07:28,563
The paper and its example checker accompany this video.

106
00:07:28,563 --> 00:07:34,744
The general theorem is a written mathematical proof; the small checks are illustrations,

107
00:07:34,744 --> 00:07:37,694
not its replacement. Valyu Research, 2026.

108
00:07:37,694 --> 00:07:40,012
Narration uses a synthetic voice.
